{
  "intro": "Hands-on CCNA labs graded in your browser: Cisco IOS-style routers and switches on a simulated network (VLANs, trunks, inter-VLAN routing, static routes, OSPF, SSH and ACLs), plus IPv6/automation drills and a wireless controller console. These are simulations, so some commands and outputs are simplified.",
  "labs": [
    {
      "id": "rtr-basics",
      "title": "Bring up a router and address its interfaces",
      "domain": "D1",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "beginner",
      "minutes": 12,
      "summary": "Name a new router, address two LAN interfaces, enable them, verify with show commands and ping, and save.",
      "scenario": "<p>A new branch router, <b>R1</b>, connects two LANs. Configure it:</p><ul><li>Hostname <code>BR1-RTR</code></li><li>G0/0 → LAN A <code>192.168.1.1/24</code> (PC-A is 192.168.1.10)</li><li>G0/1 → LAN B <code>192.168.2.1/24</code> (PC-B is 192.168.2.10)</li></ul><p>Router interfaces are shut down by default.</p>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "PC-A",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 0
          },
          {
            "name": "R1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "PC-B",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 0
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "R1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "hostname": "Router"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC-A",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.1.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.1.1"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC-B",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.2.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.2.1"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "PC-A",
          "R1 g0/0"
        ],
        [
          "R1 g0/1",
          "PC-B"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "Set the hostname to <code>BR1-RTR</code>",
          "check": {
            "dev": "R1",
            "path": "hostname",
            "eq": "BR1-RTR"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Address G0/0 as 192.168.1.1/24 and enable it",
          "check": {
            "iface": "R1 g0/0",
            "is": {
              "ip": "192.168.1.1",
              "mask": "255.255.255.0",
              "up": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Address G0/1 as 192.168.2.1/24 and enable it",
          "check": {
            "iface": "R1 g0/1",
            "is": {
              "ip": "192.168.2.1",
              "mask": "255.255.255.0",
              "up": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Check interface status with <code>show ip interface brief</code>",
          "check": {
            "ran": [
              "R1",
              "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+int"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC-A can ping PC-B through the router",
          "check": {
            "ping": [
              "PC-A",
              "192.168.2.10"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Save the configuration",
          "check": {
            "saved": "R1"
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "<code>interface g0/0</code>, <code>ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0</code>, <code>no shutdown</code>.",
        "From the router, <code>ping 192.168.2.10</code> tests the far LAN.",
        "Save with <code>copy running-config startup-config</code> or <code>write memory</code>."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>enable\nconfigure terminal\nhostname BR1-RTR\ninterface g0/0\n ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0\n no shutdown\ninterface g0/1\n ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0\n no shutdown\n end\nshow ip interface brief\nping 192.168.2.10\ncopy running-config startup-config</pre>",
      "review": "<p>A router routes between its directly connected networks as soon as interfaces are addressed and up; the hosts just need the router as their default gateway.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "trunk",
      "title": "VLANs across an 802.1Q trunk",
      "domain": "D2",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "intermediate",
      "minutes": 20,
      "summary": "Create matching VLANs on two switches, assign access ports, build a trunk with a dedicated native VLAN, and verify end-to-end reachability.",
      "scenario": "<p>Users in the same department sit on both floors. Configure <b>SW1</b> and <b>SW2</b> (link G0/1 ↔ G0/1):</p><ul><li>VLAN 10 <code>USERS</code>, VLAN 20 <code>VOICE</code>, VLAN 99 <code>NATIVE</code> on both switches</li><li>Fa0/1 on each switch: access VLAN 10 (PC1, PC3); Fa0/2 on each switch: access VLAN 20 (PC2, PC4)</li><li>G0/1 on both: trunk, native VLAN 99, allowed VLANs 10,20,99 only</li></ul>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "SW1",
            "x": 0.5,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "switch"
          },
          {
            "name": "SW2",
            "x": 2.5,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "switch"
          },
          {
            "name": "PC1",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 1
          },
          {
            "name": "PC2",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 1
          },
          {
            "name": "PC3",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 1
          },
          {
            "name": "PC4",
            "x": 3,
            "y": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "SW1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "switch",
          "interfaces": [
            "FastEthernet0/1",
            "FastEthernet0/2",
            "FastEthernet0/3",
            "FastEthernet0/4",
            "FastEthernet0/5",
            "FastEthernet0/6",
            "FastEthernet0/7",
            "FastEthernet0/8",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "SW2",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "switch",
          "interfaces": [
            "FastEthernet0/1",
            "FastEthernet0/2",
            "FastEthernet0/3",
            "FastEthernet0/4",
            "FastEthernet0/5",
            "FastEthernet0/6",
            "FastEthernet0/7",
            "FastEthernet0/8",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "PC1",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "10.10.10.11",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC2",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "10.10.20.11",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC3",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "10.10.10.12",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC4",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "10.10.20.12",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "SW1 g0/1",
          "SW2 g0/1"
        ],
        [
          "SW1 f0/1",
          "PC1"
        ],
        [
          "SW1 f0/2",
          "PC2"
        ],
        [
          "SW2 f0/1",
          "PC3"
        ],
        [
          "SW2 f0/2",
          "PC4"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "VLANs 10 <code>USERS</code>, 20 <code>VOICE</code> and 99 <code>NATIVE</code> exist on both switches",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "SW1",
                "path": "vlans.10",
                "eq": "USERS"
              },
              {
                "dev": "SW1",
                "path": "vlans.20",
                "eq": "VOICE"
              },
              {
                "dev": "SW1",
                "path": "vlans.99",
                "eq": "NATIVE"
              },
              {
                "dev": "SW2",
                "path": "vlans.10",
                "eq": "USERS"
              },
              {
                "dev": "SW2",
                "path": "vlans.20",
                "eq": "VOICE"
              },
              {
                "dev": "SW2",
                "path": "vlans.99",
                "eq": "NATIVE"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Fa0/1 is access VLAN 10 and Fa0/2 is access VLAN 20 on both switches",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "iface": "SW1 f0/1",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "access",
                  "accessVlan": 10
                }
              },
              {
                "iface": "SW1 f0/2",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "access",
                  "accessVlan": 20
                }
              },
              {
                "iface": "SW2 f0/1",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "access",
                  "accessVlan": 10
                }
              },
              {
                "iface": "SW2 f0/2",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "access",
                  "accessVlan": 20
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "G0/1 is a trunk on both switches with native VLAN 99",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "iface": "SW1 g0/1",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "trunk",
                  "nativeVlan": 99
                }
              },
              {
                "iface": "SW2 g0/1",
                "is": {
                  "mode": "trunk",
                  "nativeVlan": 99
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "The trunks allow only VLANs 10, 20 and 99",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "iface": "SW1 g0/1",
                "is": {
                  "allowed": "10,20,99"
                }
              },
              {
                "iface": "SW2 g0/1",
                "is": {
                  "allowed": "10,20,99"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Verify with <code>show interfaces trunk</code> on either switch",
          "check": {
            "any": [
              {
                "ran": [
                  "SW1",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+int(e(r(f(a(c(e(s)?)?)?)?)?)?)?\\s+tr"
                ]
              },
              {
                "ran": [
                  "SW2",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+int(e(r(f(a(c(e(s)?)?)?)?)?)?)?\\s+tr"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC1 reaches PC3 (VLAN 10) and PC2 reaches PC4 (VLAN 20) across the trunk",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "ping": [
                  "PC1",
                  "10.10.10.12"
                ]
              },
              {
                "ping": [
                  "PC2",
                  "10.10.20.12"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Configure both switches: use the device tabs above the console.",
        "<code>interface g0/1</code>, <code>switchport mode trunk</code>, <code>switchport trunk native vlan 99</code>, <code>switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,99</code>.",
        "A native VLAN mismatch between the two ends is a classic trunk problem; keep both at 99."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>! On SW1, then repeat on SW2\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nvlan 10\n name USERS\nvlan 20\n name VOICE\nvlan 99\n name NATIVE\ninterface f0/1\n switchport mode access\n switchport access vlan 10\ninterface f0/2\n switchport mode access\n switchport access vlan 20\ninterface g0/1\n switchport mode trunk\n switchport trunk native vlan 99\n switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,99\n end\nshow interfaces trunk</pre>",
      "review": "<p>Trunks carry many VLANs over one link using 802.1Q tags. Pruning the allowed list and moving the native VLAN off VLAN 1 are standard hardening steps.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "roas",
      "title": "Inter-VLAN routing with router-on-a-stick",
      "domain": "D3",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "intermediate",
      "minutes": 20,
      "summary": "Route between two VLANs using 802.1Q subinterfaces on one router port and a trunk on the switch.",
      "scenario": "<p><b>SW1</b> already has VLAN 10 (PC1, Fa0/1) and VLAN 20 (PC2, Fa0/2). Hosts can't talk across VLANs. Use router <b>R1</b>:</p><ul><li>SW1 G0/1 (to R1) as an 802.1Q trunk</li><li>R1 G0/0.10: VLAN 10, <code>192.168.10.1/24</code>; R1 G0/0.20: VLAN 20, <code>192.168.20.1/24</code></li><li>Enable the physical G0/0</li></ul>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "R1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "SW1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 1,
            "type": "switch"
          },
          {
            "name": "PC1",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 2
          },
          {
            "name": "PC2",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "R1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "SW1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "switch",
          "interfaces": [
            "FastEthernet0/1",
            "FastEthernet0/2",
            "FastEthernet0/3",
            "FastEthernet0/4",
            "FastEthernet0/5",
            "FastEthernet0/6",
            "FastEthernet0/7",
            "FastEthernet0/8",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "vlans": {
              "10": "STAFF",
              "20": "LAB"
            },
            "interfaces": {
              "FastEthernet0/1": {
                "accessVlan": 10
              },
              "FastEthernet0/2": {
                "accessVlan": 20
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "PC1",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.10.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.10.1"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC2",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.20.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.20.1"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "R1 g0/0",
          "SW1 g0/1"
        ],
        [
          "SW1 f0/1",
          "PC1"
        ],
        [
          "SW1 f0/2",
          "PC2"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "SW1 G0/1 is an 802.1Q trunk",
          "check": {
            "iface": "SW1 g0/1",
            "is": {
              "mode": "trunk"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R1 G0/0.10 uses VLAN 10 with 192.168.10.1/24",
          "check": {
            "iface": "R1 g0/0.10",
            "is": {
              "encap": 10,
              "ip": "192.168.10.1",
              "mask": "255.255.255.0"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R1 G0/0.20 uses VLAN 20 with 192.168.20.1/24",
          "check": {
            "iface": "R1 g0/0.20",
            "is": {
              "encap": 20,
              "ip": "192.168.20.1",
              "mask": "255.255.255.0"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R1 G0/0 is enabled",
          "check": {
            "iface": "R1 g0/0",
            "is": {
              "up": true
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC1 (VLAN 10) can ping PC2 (VLAN 20)",
          "check": {
            "ping": [
              "PC1",
              "192.168.20.10"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Subinterfaces: <code>interface g0/0.10</code>, then <code>encapsulation dot1Q 10</code> before the IP address.",
        "The physical port needs <code>no shutdown</code>; subinterfaces follow it.",
        "The switch side is <code>switchport mode trunk</code> on G0/1."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>! SW1\nenable\nconfigure terminal\ninterface g0/1\n switchport mode trunk\n end\n! R1\nenable\nconfigure terminal\ninterface g0/0\n no shutdown\ninterface g0/0.10\n encapsulation dot1Q 10\n ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.0\ninterface g0/0.20\n encapsulation dot1Q 20\n ip address 192.168.20.1 255.255.255.0\n end\nshow ip route</pre>",
      "review": "<p>Each subinterface is the default gateway for one VLAN. Frames arrive tagged on the trunk, the router routes between subnets, and tags them again for the destination VLAN.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "static-routes",
      "title": "Static and default routes",
      "domain": "D3",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "intermediate",
      "minutes": 15,
      "summary": "Add static routes between two routers and a default route toward the internet edge, then verify the routing tables and connectivity.",
      "scenario": "<p>The interfaces are already addressed. <b>R1</b> (branch, LAN <code>192.168.1.0/24</code>) connects to <b>R2</b> (HQ edge, LAN <code>192.168.2.0/24</code>) over <code>10.0.12.0/30</code>. R2's Loopback0 <code>203.0.113.1</code> stands in for the internet.</p><ul><li>R1: a default route toward R2 (everything not local goes to HQ)</li><li>R2: a static route back to the branch LAN</li></ul>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "PC1",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 0
          },
          {
            "name": "R1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "R2",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "PC2",
            "x": 3,
            "y": 0
          },
          {
            "name": "NET",
            "label": "Internet (Lo0)",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 1,
            "type": "cloud"
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "R1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "192.168.1.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/1": {
                "ip": "10.0.12.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "R2",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "192.168.2.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/1": {
                "ip": "10.0.12.2",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "Loopback0": {
                "ip": "203.0.113.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.255",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "PC1",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.1.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.1.1"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC2",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.2.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.2.1"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "PC1",
          "R1 g0/0"
        ],
        [
          "R1 g0/1",
          "R2 g0/1"
        ],
        [
          "R2 g0/0",
          "PC2"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "R1 has a default route (0.0.0.0/0) via 10.0.12.2",
          "check": {
            "route": [
              "R1",
              "0.0.0.0/0"
            ],
            "code": "S"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R2 has a static route to 192.168.1.0/24",
          "check": {
            "route": [
              "R2",
              "192.168.1.0/24"
            ],
            "code": "S"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Check R1's table with <code>show ip route</code>",
          "check": {
            "ran": [
              "R1",
              "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+ro"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC1 can reach PC2",
          "check": {
            "ping": [
              "PC1",
              "192.168.2.10"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC1 can reach the internet stand-in 203.0.113.1",
          "check": {
            "ping": [
              "PC1",
              "203.0.113.1"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "<code>ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.12.2</code> on R1.",
        "<code>ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.12.1</code> on R2.",
        "Routing must work in both directions; a ping needs the reply to find its way back."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>! R1\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.12.2\n end\nshow ip route\n! R2\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.12.1\n end</pre>",
      "review": "<p>A default route (the \"gateway of last resort\") is perfect for a stub branch with one way out. The HQ side still needs a specific route back, or replies never return.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "ospf",
      "title": "Single-area OSPFv2",
      "domain": "D3",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "advanced",
      "minutes": 25,
      "summary": "Enable OSPF area 0 on three routers with fixed router IDs, make the LAN ports passive, and verify neighbors and learned routes.",
      "scenario": "<p>Interfaces are addressed. Configure OSPF process 1, area 0:</p><ul><li>R1: router ID 1.1.1.1, LAN 192.168.1.0/24 (G0/1), link to R2 10.0.12.0/30 (G0/0)</li><li>R2: router ID 2.2.2.2, links 10.0.12.0/30 (G0/0) and 10.0.23.0/30 (G0/2)</li><li>R3: router ID 3.3.3.3, LAN 192.168.3.0/24 (G0/1), link to R2 10.0.23.0/30 (G0/0)</li><li>LAN interfaces on R1 and R3 should be passive</li></ul>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "PC1",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 0
          },
          {
            "name": "R1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "R2",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "R3",
            "x": 3,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "PC3",
            "x": 4,
            "y": 0
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "R1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "10.0.12.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/1": {
                "ip": "192.168.1.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "R2",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "10.0.12.2",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/2": {
                "ip": "10.0.23.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "R3",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "10.0.23.2",
                "mask": "255.255.255.252",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/1": {
                "ip": "192.168.3.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "PC1",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.1.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.1.1"
        },
        {
          "name": "PC3",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.3.10",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.3.1"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "PC1",
          "R1 g0/1"
        ],
        [
          "R1 g0/0",
          "R2 g0/0"
        ],
        [
          "R2 g0/2",
          "R3 g0/0"
        ],
        [
          "R3 g0/1",
          "PC3"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "Router IDs are 1.1.1.1, 2.2.2.2 and 3.3.3.3",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "ospf.rid",
                "eq": "1.1.1.1"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R2",
                "path": "ospf.rid",
                "eq": "2.2.2.2"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R3",
                "path": "ospf.rid",
                "eq": "3.3.3.3"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R1–R2 and R2–R3 form OSPF neighbors",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "ospfNbr": [
                  "R1",
                  "R2"
                ]
              },
              {
                "ospfNbr": [
                  "R2",
                  "R3"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "The LAN interfaces on R1 and R3 are passive",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "ospf.passive",
                "re": "GigabitEthernet0/1"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R3",
                "path": "ospf.passive",
                "re": "GigabitEthernet0/1"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "R1 learns 192.168.3.0/24 through OSPF",
          "check": {
            "route": [
              "R1",
              "192.168.3.0/24"
            ],
            "code": "O"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Check neighbors with <code>show ip ospf neighbor</code>",
          "check": {
            "any": [
              {
                "ran": [
                  "R1",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+ospf\\s+n"
                ]
              },
              {
                "ran": [
                  "R2",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+ospf\\s+n"
                ]
              },
              {
                "ran": [
                  "R3",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+ospf\\s+n"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "PC1 can ping PC3",
          "check": {
            "ping": [
              "PC1",
              "192.168.3.10"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "<code>router ospf 1</code>, <code>router-id 1.1.1.1</code>, then <code>network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0</code> and <code>network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0</code>.",
        "Wildcard = inverse of the mask: /30 → 0.0.0.3, /24 → 0.0.0.255.",
        "<code>passive-interface g0/1</code> keeps advertising the LAN but stops sending hellos there."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>! R1\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nrouter ospf 1\n router-id 1.1.1.1\n network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\n network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0\n passive-interface g0/1\n end\n! R2\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nrouter ospf 1\n router-id 2.2.2.2\n network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0\n network 10.0.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0\n end\n! R3\nenable\nconfigure terminal\nrouter ospf 1\n router-id 3.3.3.3\n network 192.168.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\n network 10.0.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0\n passive-interface g0/1\n end\nshow ip ospf neighbor\nshow ip route</pre>",
      "review": "<p>OSPF neighbors need matching area, subnet and mask on the shared link. Passive interfaces advertise the LAN without forming adjacencies on it. Routes learned from neighbors appear with code O.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "ssh-acl",
      "title": "Secure device access: SSH and a VTY ACL",
      "domain": "D4",
      "kind": "cli",
      "level": "intermediate",
      "minutes": 18,
      "summary": "Enable SSHv2 with local authentication and restrict remote management to the admin subnet with a standard ACL on the VTY lines.",
      "scenario": "<p>Router <b>R1</b> connects the admin LAN <code>192.168.10.0/24</code> (G0/0) and the user LAN <code>192.168.20.0/24</code> (G0/1); interfaces are configured. Requirements:</p><ul><li>Domain <code>corp.local</code>, 2048-bit RSA keys, SSH version 2</li><li>Local user <code>netadmin</code> with a secret, and an enable secret</li><li>VTY lines: local login, SSH only</li><li>Standard ACL 10 permitting only 192.168.10.0/24, applied to the VTY lines</li></ul>",
      "topology": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "name": "ADMIN",
            "label": "Admin PC",
            "x": 0,
            "y": 0
          },
          {
            "name": "R1",
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0,
            "type": "router"
          },
          {
            "name": "USER",
            "label": "User PC",
            "x": 2,
            "y": 0
          }
        ]
      },
      "devices": [
        {
          "name": "R1",
          "platform": "ios",
          "kind": "router",
          "interfaces": [
            "GigabitEthernet0/0",
            "GigabitEthernet0/1",
            "GigabitEthernet0/2"
          ],
          "state": {
            "interfaces": {
              "GigabitEthernet0/0": {
                "ip": "192.168.10.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              },
              "GigabitEthernet0/1": {
                "ip": "192.168.20.1",
                "mask": "255.255.255.0",
                "shutdown": false
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "ADMIN",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.10.50",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.10.1"
        },
        {
          "name": "USER",
          "platform": "host",
          "ip": "192.168.20.50",
          "mask": "255.255.255.0",
          "gw": "192.168.20.1"
        }
      ],
      "links": [
        [
          "ADMIN",
          "R1 g0/0"
        ],
        [
          "R1 g0/1",
          "USER"
        ]
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "SSH version 2 with 2048-bit keys under domain <code>corp.local</code>",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "domainName",
                "eq": "corp.local"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "rsa",
                "eq": 2048
              },
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "sshVersion",
                "eq": 2
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Local user <code>netadmin</code> (using <code>secret</code>) and an enable secret",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "users.netadmin.type",
                "eq": "secret"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "enableSecret",
                "exists": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "VTY lines use local login and SSH only",
          "check": {
            "sshReady": "R1"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "ACL 10 permits 192.168.10.0/24",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "acls.10.0.action",
                "eq": "permit"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "acls.10.0.net",
                "eq": "192.168.10.0"
              },
              {
                "dev": "R1",
                "path": "acls.10.0.wild",
                "eq": "0.0.0.255"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "ACL 10 is applied inbound on the VTY lines",
          "check": {
            "dev": "R1",
            "path": "vty.accessClass",
            "eq": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Confirm with <code>show ip ssh</code> and save",
          "check": {
            "all": [
              {
                "ran": [
                  "R1",
                  "^(do\\s+)?sh(o(w)?)?\\s+ip\\s+ssh"
                ]
              },
              {
                "saved": "R1"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Order matters for keys: hostname and <code>ip domain-name</code> first, then <code>crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048</code>.",
        "<code>access-list 10 permit 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255</code> (the implicit deny blocks everything else).",
        "In <code>line vty 0 4</code>: <code>login local</code>, <code>transport input ssh</code>, <code>access-class 10 in</code>."
      ],
      "solution": "<pre>enable\nconfigure terminal\nip domain-name corp.local\ncrypto key generate rsa modulus 2048\nip ssh version 2\nusername netadmin secret Adm1n!Pass\nenable secret Str0ng!Enable\naccess-list 10 permit 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255\nline vty 0 4\n login local\n transport input ssh\n access-class 10 in\n end\nshow ip ssh\ncopy running-config startup-config</pre>",
      "review": "<p><code>access-class</code> filters who can open a VTY session, independent of interface ACLs. Combined with SSH-only transport and local accounts, it's the standard way to lock down device management.</p>"
    },
